// expect: 42
// The two absence theorems, told apart — this leg pins TODAY'S: !Ping is
// RESIDUAL absence. An install of Ping inside a !Ping fn absorbs its own
// performs; the residual row is clean; the declaration holds and the
// program runs. The DEEP claim ("no Ping op ever executes, even
// self-absorbed") is a DIFFERENT theorem, keyed by install identity — the
// modal crown's seat (PLAN §4③). When that form lands, this leg forces the
// conscious choice: keep residual as the bare !E reading, or flip it — the
// tripwire against an accidental semantic change.
// Expected exit: 42.

effect Ping { ping() -> Int }

handler ponger {
  ping() => resume(7),
}

fn self_absorbed() -> Int with !Ping = { ping() } ~> ponger

fn main() = self_absorbed() * 6
